riables?\n\n- The average height of students in a particular class\n- The number of blue sugar-coated chocolates in a randomly selected bag of candy\n- The number of free-throws Sally makes in a carnival game where she attempts $3$ free-throws" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041842/state.json" "file_sha256" "f38ac0a7329523615058c6edd535db9049d09c549047084033bba41733014794"} {"question" "q-83758" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A random variable is a variable whose value is determined by the outcome of a trial or experiment.\n\nA *discrete* random variable is a random variable\n\n- that has finitely many possible values, or\n\n- whose possible values can be listed in a sequence: ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3},…$\n\nRemember that a real interval (e.g. $(a,b)$ or $[a,b]$) cannot be listed in a sequence.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I does *not* describe a discrete random variable. Although its value is determined by the result of an experiment, the possible values are real numbers that cannot be listed in a sequence.\n\n- Statement II describes a discrete random variable. Its value is determined by the result of an experiment, and it has finitely many possible values: $0,1,2,3,…$\n\n- Statement III describes a discrete random variable. Its value is determined by the result of an experiment, and we can list its possible values in a sequence: $0,1,2,3.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041842/state.json" "file_sha256" "f38ac0a7329523615058c6edd535db9049d09c549047084033bba41733014794"} {"question" "q-83758" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041842/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "98770bd42e2537445ea14aada000eb543290c5d4c0f5a9f71aa70e83ef6a4907"} {"question" "q-83758" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041842/state.json" "file_sha256" "f38ac0a7329523615058c6edd535db9049d09c549047084033bba41733014794"} {"question" "q-83758" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041842/state.json" "file_sha256" "f38ac0a7329523615058c6edd535db9049d09c549047084033bba41733014794"} {"question" "q-83758" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II and III only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041842/state.json" "file_sha256" "f38ac0a7329523615058c6edd535db9049d09c549047084033bba41733014794"} {"question" "e-7352" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following are discrete random variables?\n\n- The number of heads obtained when a coin is flipped $6$ times\n\n- The number of times a $6$-sided die is rolled until a $6$ appears\n\n- The number of months in a particular year" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041842/example-7352.json" "file_sha256" "dd97f64821c45d87474556d8ae3188a82e6975aea0b5f8f4b6564e666c11df1b"} {"question" "e-7352" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A random variable is a variable whose value is determined by the outcome of a trial or experiment.\n\nA *discrete* random variable is a random variable\n\n- that has finitely many possible values, or\n\n- whose possible values can be listed in a sequence: $x_1, x_2, x_3, \\ldots$\n\nRemember that a real interval (e.g. $(a,b)$ or $[a,b]$) cannot be listed in a sequence.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I describes a discrete random variable. Its value is determined by the result of an experiment, and its possible values can be listed in a sequence: $0,1,2,3,4,5,6.$\n\n- Statement II describes a discrete random variable. Its value is determined by the result of an experiment, and we can count its possible values in a sequence: $1,2,3,\\ldots$\n\n- Statement III does *not* describe a discrete random variable. The number of months in a particular year is not the outcome of a trial or experiment. (By definition, there are $12$ months in a year.)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041842/example-7352.json" "file_sha256" "dd97f64821c45d87474556d8ae3188a82e6975aea0b5f8f4b6564e666c11df1b"}] "usage" {"e-7352" []} "basis" 2327 "retractions" [[17592186105575 :question/problem "Which of the following are discrete random variables?\n\n1. The number of heads obtained when a coin is flipped $6$ times\n2. The number of times a $6$-sided die is rolled until a $6$ appears\n3. The number of months in a particular year"] [17592186105575 :question/worked-solution "A random variable is a variable whose value is determined by the outcome of a trial or experiment.\n\nA *discrete* random variable is a random variable\n\n- that has finitely many possible values, or\n- whose possible values can be listed in a sequence: $x_1, x_2, x_3, \\ldots$\n\nRemember that a real interval (e.g. $(a,b)$ or $[a,b]$) cannot be listed in a sequence.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I describes a discrete random variable. Its value is determined by the result of an experiment, and its possible values can be listed in a sequence: $0,1,2,3,4,5,6$.\n- Statement II describes a discrete random variable. Its value is determined by the result of an experiment, and we can count its possible values in a sequence: $1,2,3,\\ldots$\n- Statement III does *not* describe a discrete random variable. The number of months in a particular year is not the outcome of a trial or experiment. (By definition, there are $12$ months in a year.)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""]] "immutable_answers" [] "immutable_fields" [] "no_history" []}
